\details{
Supply two formulae, with the response in the first. The predictors
define \code{mu = exp(eta1)} and \code{sigma = exp(eta2)}. Both support offsets and
shared coefficients using \code{\link[mgcv:formula.gam]{mgcv::formula.gam()}} syntax. Use \code{~ 1} for
constant dispersion. Fitted values and response predictions have columns
\code{mu}, \code{sigma} (where mgcv preserves names); link predictions contain their
logarithms. Prediction standard errors use the delta method and the
selected coefficient covariance, including shared coefficients.

The mass at integer y >= 0 is proportional to
\code{lambda^y * Gamma(sigma) / Gamma(sigma + y)}. The internal rate lambda is
solved numerically so that \code{E(Y) = mu}; it is not an additional parameter.
sigma < 1 gives underdispersion, sigma = 1 gives Poisson, and sigma > 1
gives overdispersion. This is the HYPERPO2 parameterization of the reference.
Dispersion is not a free variance multiplier: at fixed mean its limiting
distributions range from Bernoulli (mu <= 1) or a shifted Poisson (mu > 1)
as sigma tends to zero, to geometric as sigma tends to infinity. Boundary
dispersion estimates can therefore be weakly identified. Parameters are
not artificially bounded.

REML supports outer Newton, outer BFGS, and extended Fellner--Schall
(\code{optimizer = "efs"}). NCV and QNCV use BFGS, including default leave-one-out
or explicit \code{nei} neighbourhoods. EFS selects REML and requires all smoothing
parameters free and unlinked, or all fixed; use an outer optimizer for
partly fixed or linked smoothing parameters. EFS uses gamma = 1 as in mgcv.
ML, \code{bam()} and \code{gamm()} are unsupported. On mgcv 1.9-3, fixed smoothing
parameters with a later intercept-only predictor may require
\code{G <- gam(..., fit = FALSE)} followed by \code{gam(G = G, sp = ...)}.

Responses must be finite nonnegative integers. Weights must be finite and
nonnegative, with positive total and at least one positive count among
positive-weight observations. Weights multiply the likelihood and all its
derivatives. Missing rows follow \code{gam()}'s \code{na.action}. Additional likelihood
scale is fixed at one. On mgcv 1.9-4, fully unpenalized NCV/QNCV models
require explicit \code{nei} with \code{jackknife = FALSE} to avoid an upstream
empty-penalty covariance error.

Native \code{rd(mu, wt, scale)},
\code{cdf(q, mu, wt, scale, logp = FALSE, lower.tail = TRUE)}, and
\code{qf(p, mu, wt, scale, lower.tail = TRUE, log.p = FALSE)} callbacks consume
a two-column response-scale parameter matrix. Probability arguments are
scalar or one per row. Missing inputs propagate; invalid inputs are
rejected. Zero mean is allowed as a point mass at zero in these callbacks.
For positive means, lower-tail quantiles at zero and one are zero and
infinity. Weights and external scale do not alter the simulated distribution.
The native \code{gratia_diagnostics()} factory supplies response moments,
discrete CDF left limits, probabilities, quantiles, and simulation.

Deviance compares with the saturated mean y, holding dispersion fixed.
Null deviance fits a constant mean predictor, retaining its offsets and
fitted dispersion; without a mean intercept it uses the offset alone.
These diagnostics are conditional on dispersion. Response residuals are
\code{y - mu}; Pearson residuals use the response variance and square-root weights.

The pure-R implementation uses adaptive summation, safeguarded mean
inversion and analytic Taylor derivatives through fourth order. Requested
tails extend beyond the normalization support when necessary. Extreme
parameters can exhaust the numerical controls and cause an explicit error.
Mathematical details are in \code{inst/maths/hyper-poissonls.md}.
}
\section{NCV neighbourhood preprocessing}{

In mgcv 1.9-4, model-frame omission can corrupt explicit \code{nei}
neighbourhoods before fitting. This is shared mgcv preprocessing, separate
from likelihood, deletion-update, QNCV and covariance restrictions. This
family checks the adjusted structure in \code{gam()}'s general-family \code{presetup}
hook and rejects inconsistent indices or cumulative group boundaries, also
when \code{nei} requests NCV calculations during REML. The check is not tied to
a presumed fixed mgcv version. Correctly adjusted whole-group omissions,
complete-data neighbourhoods and default leave-one-out (\code{nei = NULL})
constructed after omission remain available within the family's supported
methods. Ordinary REML without \code{nei} is unaffected.

Remove incomplete observations using all relevant response, predictor,
offset and weight variables, apply subset selection, then construct \code{nei}
using the resulting data's row indices. Deletion and prediction sets may
differ; their ordering, repetitions and grouping are not changed by inlaws.
If an incomplete \code{nei} list fails inside mgcv's adjustment itself, it fails
before any family hook and cannot receive the inlaws diagnostic. See the
family compatibility vignette for route and version qualifications.
}

\examples{
set.seed(104)
d <- data.frame(x = runif(120))
f <- hyper_poissonls()
d$y <- f$rd(cbind(exp(1 + d$x), exp(-0.3 + d$x)))
b <- mgcv::gam(list(y ~ x, ~ x), data = d, family = f, method = "REML")
head(predict(b, type = "response"))
head(b$family$cdf(d$y, fitted(b)))
}
